from flask import Flask, Response

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.after_request
def deny_access(response):
    # Set the status code to 403 Forbidden
    response.status_code = 403
    # Set the 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header to deny access from all origins
    response.headers['Access-Control-Allow-Origin'] = 'http://example.com'
    return response

@app.route('/')
def homepage():
    # Return a simple homepage with a 403 Forbidden status code
    return Response("This website is forbidden.", status=403)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run(debug=True)
```

In this script, the `deny_access` function is called after every request to the server. It modifies the response by setting the status code to 403 Forbidden and the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header to 'http://example.com', which tells the browser to deny access from any origin other than 'http://example.com'. When a user tries to access the homepage, they will receive a response with a 403 status code, effectively denying access to the website or service.

